<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#born-in-the-purple">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Born in the purple</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as born to the purple, Porphyrogennetos, Porphyrogenitus, Porphyrogenita</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Born in the purple was a category of members of royal families born while their parent reigned, later extended to children of any prominent parents. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> the honorific porphyrogennetos, Latinized Porphyrogenitus, was given to a son or daughter born after the father had become emperor; the Byzantines linked it to the imperial purple or to the porphyry-lined room of the Great Palace where empresses gave birth. The idea was known from the 6th century, but the word is first securely used in 846 and was common by the 10th century, notably for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a>. Porphyrogeniture favoured such sons over older brothers, as Henry I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> argued against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-curthose">Robert Curthose</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/born-in-the-purple.jpg" alt="Born in the purple" width="330" height="484" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photographer: Myrabella; derivative work by Myrabella · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zoe_mosaic_Hagia_Sophia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In principle the Senate, the army and the people acclaimed each emperor; in practice emperors crowned their sons as co-emperors in childhood, and a son born to a reigning emperor in the palace’s purple chamber was called porphyrogennetos, “<b>born in the purple</b>”.</blockquote><a href="/societies/byzantine-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Byzantine Empire</a></li></ul></section>
